SDC methods applied: Primary cell suppression only. Any cell — a given day/week/month/year combined with an origin and/or destination region — built from 10 or fewer underlying source transaction rows has every measure in that row replaced with the character "c", and a disclosure_status column states plainly which rows were suppressed and why. No rounding or perturbation has been applied to the non-suppressed values. I'll flag this myself rather than wait to be asked: this is primary suppression only — I haven't applied secondary/complementary suppression (protecting against a masked cell being inferred from a published row or column total), and I'd welcome the review team's steer on whether that's needed for tables structured this way, particularly the ones with regional totals.

Variable names that aren't self-explanatory:

origin_region_code/origin_region_name, destination_region_code/destination_region_name — ITL1 (formerly NUTS1) region of the journey's start and end. "UNKNOWN"/"Unknown region" means no region could be assigned to that end of the journey from the source data.
net_passenger_journeys — count of passenger journeys, net of refunds (a refund subtracts from the count it originally added).
net_tickets — count of ticket sales, net of refunds. A secondary measure — differs from journeys because one ticket (e.g. a return) can represent more than one journey.
total_passenger_miles — total passenger-miles travelled in that row.
avg_journey_miles — average distance per passenger journey (total_passenger_miles ÷ net_passenger_journeys).
avg_passenger_miles_per_ticket — average passenger-miles per ticket sold (a different denominator — total_passenger_miles ÷ row_count).
commute_like_journeys/leisure_like_journeys — journeys split by a provisional 30-mile-per-journey threshold, pending a firmer definition from the stakeholder. Not a final classification.
is_within_region — true where the origin and destination region are the same.
row_count — number of underlying source rows behind that cell; used to decide suppression, not a journey count itself.
journeys_index (indexed files only) — that region's journey count in the given period, as a percentage of that same region's count in the base period (index_base_period states which period). Regions are each indexed against their own baseline, so index values aren't comparable across regions in absolute terms — only trend-over-time within one region.
